Look at **Chart 1**. Besides the species, the **first hominids** and **humans** are highlighted. These are not the only groups used to understand our evolution. So we decided to explain them to avoid confusion (<htcite0>):
- **Hominids**: This is the biggest group. It has us and other extinct hominin species, and also the great apes, like gorillas and chimpanzees;
- **Hominins**: Include all human groups (genus *Homo*), and also *Australopithecus*, *Paranthropus*, and *Ardipithecus*;
- **Humans**: Are all extinct and living species of the genus *Homo*, but not *Australopithecus*, *Paranthropus*, and *Ardipithecus*.

history.text:
Finally, it is time to talk about us, *Homo sapiens*. But before we start, we need to point out some things about other species and about us:
- Our species is the eleventh species of the genus *Homo* that we will study;
- As *Graph 2* and the whole text show, our species did not appear on Earth at the same time as the others;
- As we can see in many figures on this page, the body structure of the species of the genus *Homo* is similar. This is because we share DNA, and we also share DNA with other species, like chimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*) (<htcite0>);
- We can see that DNA is shared with other species when we can take DNA from fossils of species that went extinct recently (<htcite1>). When we cannot take it anymore, we can still compare the final result: the fossils;
- People only understood DNA in the 20th century (<htcite2>), according to the Gregorian calendar. But this did not stop people like Charles Darwin (<htcite3>; <htcite4>; <htcite5>) from saying, before DNA was found, what DNA later confirmed;
- About how our knowledge and beliefs grew, we must say something about the indigenous peoples of Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*). We cannot call them users of the scientific method. But they could see the link between us and other monkeys in their way of seeing the world (<htcite6>; <htcite7>).
- Finally, whatever the features of the 11 species of the genus *Homo*, millions of years ago we separated from other species, in a process of speciation. This process went on because of genetic mutations. And we, *Homo sapiens*, who now live on the whole planet, come from Africa (<htcite8>).

Our human species, *Homo sapiens*, has other names that were given as our knowledge grew (<htcite0>; <htcite1>):
- *Homo sapiens sapiens*: this name appears when we compare our species with other species. Some people use it because they think *Homo sapiens* is a bigger group — a group that could even include Neanderthals. In that case, Neanderthals would be called *Homo sapiens neanderthalensis*;
- **Arcaíco**: a name for our ancestors who lived in Africa between <htdate0> and <htdate1>;
- **Cro-Magnon**: a name for humans who lived in Europe between <htdate2> and <htdate3>.
